**Benchmark reality (JMH results, typical 8-core machine):**

```
Operation             for-loop     stream      parallelStream
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
sum 10 ints           3 ns         12 ns       45,000 ns
sum 10K ints          8 μs         11 μs       15 μs
sum 1M ints           0.8 ms       1.1 ms      0.3 ms       ← parallel wins!
filter+map+reduce 1M  2.1 ms       2.3 ms      0.5 ms       ← parallel wins!
groupingBy 100K       15 ms        14 ms       6 ms         ← parallel wins!
LinkedList sum 100K   12 ms        13 ms       18 ms        ← parallel SLOWER!
```

**Why streams have overhead:**

```java
// For-loop: direct array access, primitive ops
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) sum += arr[i];  // 0 object creation

// Stream: pipeline setup + lambda + potential autoboxing
int sum = IntStream.of(arr).sum();                      // avoids boxing with IntStream
int sum = Arrays.stream(arr).boxed().reduce(0, Integer::sum);  // BOXING! Integer objects!
```

**When for-loop wins:**

```java
// 1. Primitive-heavy (avoids autoboxing):
int[] prices = new int[1_000_000];
int sum = 0;
for (int p : prices) sum += p;  // pure primitives — fastest

// 2. Index-based access:
for (int i = 1; i < trades.size(); i++) {
    if (trades.get(i).getAmount().compareTo(trades.get(i-1).getAmount()) > 0) { ... }
}

// 3. Simple single-step iteration:
for (Trade t : trades) { t.setStatus("PROCESSED"); }
```

**When stream matches/wins:**

```java
// 1. Multi-step pipeline (fusion compensates overhead):
trades.stream()
    .filter(Trade::isSettled)
    .map(Trade::getAmount)
    .sorted()
    .limit(10)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
// Stream: ONE fused pass (filter+map per element) + sort + limit
// For-loop: would need separate passes or complex manual code

// 2. Parallel on large CPU-bound data:
portfolios.parallelStream()
    .map(this::monteCarloSimulation)  // CPU-intensive per element
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

